window.BOOK_FC=[{"t":"Absolute and relative risk","s":14,"e":18,"c":[{"q":"A trial reports relapse in 48% on placebo and 30% on lithium. What is the relative risk reduction?","a":"37.5%. RR = 0.30 / 0.48 = 0.625, so lithium removes 37.5% of the placebo risk. The absolute gap is only 18 points; the relative figure always looks bigger.","p":15,"f":"RRR = 1 − RR = (CER − EER) / CER"},{"q":"In the same trial (48% placebo, 30% lithium), what is the absolute risk reduction and what does it mean in people?","a":"18 percentage points. Subtract the rates: 48% − 30%. That is 18 fewer relapses for every 100 people treated for a year.","p":15,"f":"ARR = CER − EER"},{"q":"A trainee reads 'relapse occurred in 20% on treatment' as a 20% risk reduction. What is the error?","a":"An event rate is not a reduction. 20% is one arm's risk. A reduction needs two rates, compared by subtraction (ARR) or division (RR, then RRR). Write CER and EER down first.","p":14},{"q":"A drug has RR 0.6 in every population. Baseline relapse is 15% in first-episode and 60% in recurrent patients. What are the ARRs?","a":"6 and 24 points. Treated risks are 9% and 36%. The relative effect stays constant, but absolute benefit grows with baseline risk, so high-risk patients gain most.","p":16,"f":"ARR = CER × (1 − RR)"},{"q":"What are the null values for RR, OR, ARR and RRR, and why does it matter when reading confidence intervals?","a":"1 for RR and OR; 0 for ARR and RRR. A result is significant only if its whole 95% CI sits on one side of the null, so the wrong null gives the wrong verdict.","p":16},{"q":"A small trial reports an RRR of +25% with 95% CI −58% to +64%. How should you interpret it?","a":"Inconclusive, not negative. The interval crosses 0, the null for an RRR, and is wide enough to fit large benefit or real harm. The trial was too small to tell.","p":17},{"q":"Clozapine response is 32/80 versus 16/80 on a comparator antipsychotic. What are the absolute benefit increase and the relative risk?","a":"ABI 0.20; RR 2.0. The rates are 0.40 and 0.20. Response is a good outcome, so the absolute effect is a benefit increase, and response is twice as likely.","p":17,"f":"ABI = EER − CER; RR = EER / CER"},{"q":"Marked sedation occurs in 36/80 on clozapine versus 12/80 on the comparator. Name and calculate the absolute effect.","a":"Absolute risk increase of 0.30. The rates are 0.45 and 0.15. A harmful outcome that rises with treatment is a risk increase; the relative version is the relative risk increase.","p":17,"f":"ARI = EER − CER"},{"q":"Why should you not quote a relative risk reduction alone when explaining a treatment to a patient?","a":"It hides the baseline risk. A 50% RRR can mean 2 in 100 falling to 1 in 100. Give absolute risks with and without treatment, over a stated timeframe.","p":16},{"q":"When the outcome is desirable, such as remission, what terms replace absolute and relative risk reduction?","a":"Absolute and relative benefit increase (ABI, RBI). 'Risk reduction' describes fewer bad events. When good events rise it is a benefit increase; when bad events rise it is a risk increase (ARI, RRI).","p":16}]},{"t":"NNT and NNH","s":19,"e":23,"c":[{"q":"Remission is 32% on an antidepressant and 21% on placebo at 8 weeks. What is the NNT?","a":"10. ABI = 11%, and 1 / 0.11 = 9.09, rounded up. Treat 10 people for 8 weeks rather than placebo and one extra will remit.","p":20,"f":"NNT = 1 / ABI"},{"q":"Why is an NNT of 9.09 reported as 10, not 9?","a":"Always round up. You cannot treat part of a person, and rounding down overstates the benefit. Rounding up keeps the estimate conservative, never flattering the treatment.","p":20},{"q":"An NNT of 10 is quoted with nothing else. What three things must accompany it to be meaningful?","a":"Comparator, outcome and timeframe. NNT 10 against placebo for remission over 8 weeks is a different claim from NNT 10 over a year. Without them the number cannot be interpreted.","p":20},{"q":"A drug's RRR is fixed at 40%. What happens to the NNT if your patient's baseline risk is double that of the trial controls?","a":"It halves. NNT is inversely proportional to baseline risk at a fixed RRR, so the same drug can have an NNT of 5 in a high-risk group and 50 in a low-risk group.","p":20,"f":"NNT = 1 / (CER × RRR)"},{"q":"A trial NNT is 12. Your patient is judged three times as likely to relapse as the trial controls. What is their individual NNT?","a":"4. Divide the trial NNT by f, the patient's risk relative to trial controls: 12 / 3 = 4. The higher the patient's risk, the fewer need treating for one to benefit.","p":21,"f":"NNT(patient) = NNT / f"},{"q":"ARR is 12% with 95% CI 3.2% to 20.8%. What is the 95% CI for the NNT?","a":"About 4.8 to 31 (point NNT 9). Invert each limit and the ends swap: the upper ARR limit gives the lower NNT. The interval is lopsided because of the reciprocal.","p":21,"f":"NNT limits = 1 / 0.208 to 1 / 0.032"},{"q":"An ARR is 5% with 95% CI −3.7% to 13.7%. How is the matching NNT interval described?","a":"From NNTB 7.3 through infinity to NNTH 27. The ARR interval crosses zero, so the NNT interval passes through infinity (no effect) and spans both benefit and harm.","p":21,"f":"1 / 0.137 = 7.3; 1 / 0.037 = 27"},{"q":"Response on an SSRI is 50% versus 40% on placebo; new self-harm thoughts 5% versus 3%. Calculate the likelihood of being helped versus harmed.","a":"LHH = 5. NNT is 10 and NNH is 50, so a young person is five times more likely to be helped than harmed this way. Weight it by how serious the harm is.","p":22,"f":"LHH = NNH / NNT = ABI / ARI"},{"q":"Drug M causes akathisia in 24/160 versus 8/160 on placebo over six weeks. What is the NNH?","a":"10. ARI = 0.15 − 0.05 = 0.10, and 1 / 0.10 = 10. Treat 10 people with drug M for six weeks and one extra will develop akathisia.","p":22,"f":"NNH = 1 / ARI"},{"q":"A trainee gets an NNT of 2.5 by inverting a relative risk reduction of 40%. What is wrong?","a":"The NNT inverts only an absolute difference. The RRR ignores baseline risk, so inverting it, or an event rate, is meaningless. Find CER and EER, subtract, then invert.","p":20}]},{"t":"Odds ratios versus relative risks","s":24,"e":28,"c":[{"q":"On an SSRI 60 of 100 respond; on placebo 30 of 100. What is the odds ratio for response?","a":"3.5. The odds are 60/40 = 1.5 and 30/70 = 0.43. The cross-product shortcut gives (60 × 70) / (40 × 30) = 3.5.","p":25,"f":"OR = ad / bc"},{"q":"In that trial the OR is 3.5. Can you say patients were 3.5 times as likely to respond?","a":"No. The RR is 0.60 / 0.30 = 2. Response is common, so the OR lies further from 1 than the RR, never between them. 'Times as likely' fits an OR only for rare outcomes.","p":25},{"q":"Distinguish risk from odds, and convert a probability of 0.5 into odds.","a":"Risk is events over everyone; odds are events over non-events. A probability of 0.5 gives odds of 0.5 / 0.5 = 1, or evens.","p":25,"f":"odds = p / (1 − p); p = odds / (1 + odds)"},{"q":"Below roughly what outcome frequency does the odds ratio approximate the relative risk, and why?","a":"About 10%. When events are rare, non-events almost equal the total, so odds approximate risk. With RR 2, an outcome at 1.5% gives OR 2.03; one at 30% gives 3.5.","p":27},{"q":"An OR of 3.5 is reported for response, with placebo response at 30%. Convert it to an approximate relative risk.","a":"RR ≈ 2.0. 3.5 / (1 − 0.3 + 0.3 × 3.5) = 3.5 / 1.75 = 2.0. The conversion shows how much a common outcome inflates the odds ratio.","p":25,"f":"RR ≈ OR / (1 − P₀ + P₀ × OR)"},{"q":"Why can you not calculate incidence or relative risk from a case-control study?","a":"The investigator fixes how many cases and controls are recruited, so the proportion with the outcome reflects the design, not the true risk. Only the odds ratio survives this sampling.","p":25},{"q":"A study follows cannabis users and non-users for five years to see who develops psychosis. What measure of association does it give naturally?","a":"Relative risk. It is a cohort: sampled by exposure and followed forward, it gives true incidence in each group, and their ratio is the RR. Case-control designs sample by outcome and give ORs.","p":26},{"q":"In a case-control study, anxiety disorder is present in 30 of 100 heavy phone users and 20 of 200 light users. What is the OR?","a":"3.86. (30 × 180) / (70 × 20) = 5400 / 1400. Incidence cannot be calculated, because cases and controls were sampled.","p":28,"f":"OR = ad / bc"},{"q":"If the same phone-use table came from a two-year cohort, what is the relative risk, and why does it differ from the OR?","a":"RR 3.0: risks are 30/100 = 0.30 and 20/200 = 0.10. Anxiety is not rare among heavy users, so the OR (3.86) sits further from 1.","p":28,"f":"RR = [a / (a + b)] / [c / (c + d)]"},{"q":"Which methods produce odds ratios, and on what scale should ORs be plotted?","a":"Case-control studies and logistic regression. Like RRs, ORs have a null value of 1 and belong on a log scale, where 0.5 and 2 sit equally far from 1.","p":24}]},{"t":"Communicating risk and shared decisions","s":29,"e":34,"c":[{"q":"In an icon array of 100 people continuing an antidepressant, 18 relapse anyway and 22 avoid relapse. What is the NNT?","a":"5. Without treatment 40 in 100 relapse; with it, 18. The ARR is 22 per 100, so NNT = 100 / 22 = 4.55, rounded up to 5.","p":30,"f":"NNT = 100 / helped icons"},{"q":"In the same icon array, what is the relative risk reduction, and why should it come second?","a":"55% (22 / 40). True, but 'over half' sounds far bigger than '22 in 100'. Relative figures inflate perceived benefit and hide baseline risk, so lead with absolute risks.","p":31,"f":"RRR = ARR / CER"},{"q":"What does an icon array (Cates plot) show that a single effect size hides?","a":"Every group: those helped, harmed, relapsing anyway and unaffected. Each icon is one person and one percentage point, so the unaffected majority, often forgotten, becomes visible.","p":31},{"q":"A leaflet gives one side effect as '1 in 25' and another as '1 in 40'. What is the problem, and the fix?","a":"Mixed denominators make comparison hard. Put both over the same number: 4 per 100 versus 2.5 per 100. The larger risk is then obvious at a glance.","p":31},{"q":"Relapse is 57 in 100 after stopping an antipsychotic and 26 in 100 after continuing. What are the ARR and NNT?","a":"ARR 31 percentage points; NNT 4 (100 / 31 = 3.2, rounded up). The relative reduction, 54%, sounds bigger but describes the same result.","p":32,"f":"NNT = 100 / ARR%"},{"q":"Screening 1,000 postnatal women: prevalence 10%, sensitivity 80%, false positive rate 10%. What proportion of positives truly have depression?","a":"About 47%: 80 true positives and 90 false positives, so 80 / 170. Natural frequencies turn this into counting; conditional probabilities tempt people to answer 80%, the sensitivity.","p":33,"f":"PPV = TP / (TP + FP)"},{"q":"What cognitive error do natural frequency trees correct?","a":"Base-rate neglect. With conditional probabilities people ignore prevalence and confuse sensitivity with predictive value. Counting people from one population keeps the base rate in view.","p":29},{"q":"What is the framing effect, and how do you counter it when presenting options?","a":"The same facts worded differently, such as '90 in 100 stay well' versus '10 in 100 relapse', change choices. Counter it by giving both frames and both absolute risks.","p":29},{"q":"Why are verbal descriptors such as 'common' or 'rare' not enough on their own?","a":"People read them very differently, so one word conveys a wide range of risks. Pair them with numbers, ideally natural frequencies over a stated timeframe.","p":31},{"q":"Name the three stages of shared decision-making described in NICE NG197.","a":"Team talk, option talk, decision talk. Agree a choice exists, lay out options with absolute risks and decision aids, then decide together on what matters to the person, checking understanding.","p":29}]},{"t":"Appraising a therapy paper","s":35,"e":40,"c":[{"q":"What are the five steps of evidence-based practice, and the three questions for appraising a therapy trial?","a":"Ask (PICO), acquire, appraise, apply, assess. Appraisal asks: are the results valid, what are they, and will they help my patient?","p":35},{"q":"A trial shows an antipsychotic lowers prolactin as hoped. Why is that weak evidence of patient benefit?","a":"Prolactin is a surrogate outcome. Changes in markers, receptor occupancy or rating-scale scores alone may not translate into what patients value, such as relapse, function or quality of life.","p":37},{"q":"Response is 54/120 versus 42/120 as randomised, but 54/100 versus 42/108 among completers. What is wrong with the completers-only analysis?","a":"It breaks randomisation and inflates benefit: 15.1 versus 10 points. Dropouts, often those doing worse or with side effects, leave unevenly, so the remaining groups are no longer comparable.","p":36},{"q":"Define intention-to-treat analysis and the question it answers.","a":"Analyse everyone in the arm they were randomised to, whatever happened later. It preserves randomisation and answers: what happens if I offer this treatment? Per-protocol analysis is closer to efficacy but prone to bias.","p":36},{"q":"20 of 120 randomised to drug and 12 of 120 to placebo are lost. Calculate attrition and say what else to check.","a":"17% versus 10%. Check whether losses are balanced and their reasons similar. Here 14 drug dropouts were for adverse effects, a reason linked to the outcome.","p":37,"f":"attrition = losses after randomisation / number randomised"},{"q":"Distinguish allocation concealment from blinding.","a":"Concealment hides the next allocation before and during assignment, preventing selection bias. Blinding hides the treatment received afterwards, preventing performance and detection bias. Concealment is always possible; blinding is not.","p":37},{"q":"In a mood stabiliser trial, good adherers relapse less than poor adherers. Why does this not show the drug works?","a":"Good adherers do better even on placebo (28% versus 45%). Adherence marks a better prognosis and confounds the comparison. The fair test compares the randomised arms as a whole.","p":38},{"q":"Trial X: ARR +2.0 points (95% CI −3.1 to +7.1); the minimal important difference is 10 points. Interpret it.","a":"Truly negative. The whole interval lies below 10, so any benefit is too small to matter. A non-significant trial is negative only if its CI excludes an important benefit.","p":39},{"q":"In a family therapy trial, the assessor rating eating attitudes knows which families had therapy. Name the bias and the remedy.","a":"Detection bias; blind the outcome assessors. Knowing the allocation can colour ratings, and assessors can be blinded even when therapists and families cannot.","p":39},{"q":"In a CBT trial, only the experimental arm also gets support-worker visits. What bias is this, and what safeguard was missing?","a":"Performance bias from unequal co-interventions. Any difference may reflect the extra care, not CBT. Groups should receive equal care apart from the intervention.","p":40}]},{"t":"Systematic reviews and meta-analysis","s":41,"e":46,"c":[{"q":"Searching 'lithium' and 'suicide', which Boolean operators retrieve the intersection, the union, and lithium records without suicide?","a":"AND, OR and NOT respectively. AND narrows; OR broadens and combines synonyms; NOT excludes but can lose relevant papers, so use it sparingly.","p":45},{"q":"What is secondary research, and how does a narrative review differ from a systematic one?","a":"Secondary research recruits studies, not people. A narrative review picks studies by the author's familiarity and cannot be reproduced; a systematic review uses a protocol and an explicit, reproducible search and selection.","p":41},{"q":"A PRISMA diagram shows 2,817 database and 46 register records, with 941 duplicates removed. How many were screened by title and abstract?","a":"1,922. Identified records total 2,863; removing duplicates leaves 1,922. Of these, 1,838 were excluded at screening, leaving 84 full texts sought.","p":42,"f":"screened = identified − duplicates"},{"q":"List the key steps of a systematic review in order.","a":"PICO question, registered protocol (e.g. PROSPERO), comprehensive search with grey literature, duplicate screening and extraction, risk of bias assessment, synthesis, GRADE certainty rating, PRISMA reporting.","p":43},{"q":"A review searched only MEDLINE, in English. Why is that a flaw?","a":"The search is incomplete and biased towards positive results. Use several databases, trial registers, grey literature (theses, conference abstracts, reports), reference lists and experts.","p":43},{"q":"Does a meta-analysis of biased trials give a trustworthy answer?","a":"No: garbage in, garbage out. Pooling adds precision, not validity, so biased studies give a precise biased estimate. Assess risk of bias before you pool.","p":43},{"q":"How are trials weighted in a fixed-effect meta-analysis, and what should never decide the weight?","a":"By inverse variance: trials with more participants and events have narrower CIs and more weight. Never weight by journal prestige or the size of the result.","p":44,"f":"weight = 1 / variance"},{"q":"A pooled RR for relapse is 0.64 (95% CI 0.53 to 0.76), though two small trials cross 1. Interpret it.","a":"About a 36% relative reduction in relapse, and significant because the CI excludes 1. The small trials crossed 1 for lack of precision, which pooling supplies.","p":44,"f":"RRR = 1 − RR"},{"q":"Is a pooled estimate the simple average of the trials' point estimates?","a":"No. It is a weighted average of the log risk ratios, using inverse-variance weights, so the most precise trials pull the diamond towards their own estimates.","p":44},{"q":"Trials measure depression on different rating scales. What effect measure allows pooling, and what rule applies to study designs?","a":"The standardised mean difference, valid when the scales measure the same construct. Pool like designs together rather than mixing, say, trials with observational studies.","p":41}]},{"t":"Forest plots and line of no effect","s":47,"e":52,"c":[{"q":"On a forest plot of risk ratios, where is the line of no effect and why is the axis usually logarithmic?","a":"At 1. Ratios are multiplicative, so a log axis makes halving (0.5) and doubling (2) sit equally far from 1, keeping benefit and harm symmetrical.","p":47},{"q":"A forest plot shows a pooled standardised mean difference. Where is the line of no effect?","a":"At 0. Any difference measure (MD, SMD, risk difference) has its null at zero; only ratios use 1. Placing it at 1 is a classic error.","p":50},{"q":"A trial's risk ratio is 0.81 (95% CI 0.60 to 1.08). Its square sits left of 1. Is it statistically significant?","a":"No. The interval crosses 1, so 'no difference' cannot be excluded. Significance depends on the whole CI clearing the line, not where the square lands.","p":48},{"q":"Eight trials: only three individually significant, yet the pooled RR is 0.74 (0.65 to 0.85). How can that be?","a":"Pooling adds precision. Most trials point the same way but are too small alone; combined, the interval narrows and the diamond lies wholly left of 1.","p":48},{"q":"Trial X has SE 0.1 and trial Y has SE 0.2. Under inverse-variance pooling, how many times more weight does X get?","a":"Four times. Weight is 1/SE², so 1/0.01 = 100 versus 1/0.04 = 25. Halving the standard error quadruples the weight.","p":48,"f":"weight = 1 / SE²"},{"q":"What do the centre and the two tips of the diamond on a forest plot tell you?","a":"The centre is the pooled point estimate; the left and right tips are the limits of its 95% confidence interval.","p":48},{"q":"A candidate says the trial with the longest horizontal line has the biggest effect. What is wrong?","a":"Line length shows precision, not effect size. A long line means a wide CI from a small or imprecise trial; the effect is the square's position on the axis.","p":49},{"q":"Trials measured depression with HAM-D, MADRS and BDI-II. Which summary measure lets them be pooled, and how is its size judged?","a":"Standardised mean difference (e.g. Hedges g): difference divided by SD, removing the scale units. Roughly 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large.","p":47},{"q":"Pooled PHQ-9 mean difference is −2.7 (95% CI −3.3 to −2.1), behavioural activation versus waiting list. Interpret it.","a":"Behavioural activation lowers PHQ-9 by about 2.7 points more than waiting list, significantly, as the CI excludes 0. It is scale points, not per cent.","p":50},{"q":"One trial's line ends in an arrowhead. What does that signal, and is the trial dropped from the pooled estimate?","a":"Its CI runs off the axis: a very imprecise, usually small trial. It stays in the analysis but carries little weight, shown by a tiny square.","p":51}]},{"t":"Heterogeneity, I² and fixed vs random effects","s":53,"e":59,"c":[{"q":"Define statistical heterogeneity in a meta-analysis.","a":"Variation between study results greater than chance alone would produce. It suggests the trials are not all estimating one identical true effect.","p":53},{"q":"Ten trials give Cochran's Q = 29.7. Calculate I² and classify it.","a":"About 70%: (29.7 − 9) / 29.7. Df is k − 1 = 9. Roughly 70% of the spread reflects real differences; Cochrane bands call this substantial.","p":55,"f":"I² = (Q − df) / Q × 100%"},{"q":"Why is Cochran's Q often judged at p < 0.10 rather than 0.05?","a":"With few trials Q has low power, so a non-significant result does not prove homogeneity. A looser threshold reduces the chance of missing real heterogeneity.","p":56},{"q":"Why is I² preferred over Q for comparing heterogeneity between meta-analyses of different sizes?","a":"Q tends to rise with the number of trials, whereas I² is a percentage that does not depend on k, so it compares fairly.","p":56},{"q":"Q is highly significant. A trainee concludes 'the treatment works'. Spot the flaw.","a":"Q tests whether trials disagree with each other, not whether the pooled effect differs from zero. Efficacy is read from the diamond's CI.","p":53},{"q":"How do fixed-effect and random-effects models differ in assumptions and output?","a":"Fixed: one true effect, differences are chance. Random: true effects vary (variance τ²), so the CI is wider and small trials gain relative weight. Identical when τ² = 0.","p":55,"f":"random-effects weight = 1 / (SEᵢ² + τ²)"},{"q":"Name the three types of heterogeneity and say which can be measured statistically.","a":"Clinical (participants, interventions, outcomes), methodological (design, bias, analysis) and statistical. Only statistical is measured; the others are judged by reading the studies.","p":55},{"q":"Studies of 'family intervention' range from one forensic session to two years of CAMHS therapy, with unrelated outcomes. What should the reviewers do?","a":"Not pool; write a narrative synthesis. Clinical heterogeneity this wide means one pooled number answers no real question, even if I² were 0%.","p":58},{"q":"Splitting trials by EPA versus DHA formulation removes heterogeneity (interaction p < 0.001). Is EPA proven superior?","a":"No. Subgroups across trials are observational, not randomised; other differences may explain it. Credibility needs pre-specification, few subgroups, plausibility and replication.","p":58},{"q":"One trial's effect is ten times larger than the rest. What is Cochrane's first step before exploring heterogeneity?","a":"Check the data. A standard error entered as a standard deviation shrinks the spread and inflates a standardised mean difference.","p":59}]},{"t":"Publication bias and funnel plots","s":60,"e":65,"c":[{"q":"What is publication bias, and in which direction does it usually push a pooled estimate?","a":"Publication depending on a study's result. Small null trials stay in the file drawer, so pooled effects look larger than the truth.","p":60},{"q":"What goes on each axis of a funnel plot, and what pattern suggests no bias?","a":"Effect on x, standard error on y (inverted, large trials at top). With no bias, points form a symmetrical inverted funnel around the pooled estimate.","p":61},{"q":"A funnel plot of odds ratios for response has an empty lower-left corner. What does it suggest?","a":"Small trials showing little or no benefit are missing, the fingerprint of publication bias. Small trials appear only when showing large benefit.","p":61},{"q":"A funnel plot is clearly asymmetric. Can you conclude publication bias?","a":"No. Asymmetry shows small-study effects. Other causes include genuine heterogeneity, poorer methods in small trials, chance and artefact.","p":62},{"q":"Why should funnel plot asymmetry tests not be run on a meta-analysis of six trials?","a":"With fewer than about 10 studies, tests such as Egger's have very low power, so a negative result is uninformative.","p":62},{"q":"Egger's test reports an intercept of 1.56 (95% CI 0.19 to 2.94). Interpret it.","a":"Significant asymmetry: the CI excludes 0, so small, imprecise trials report larger effects than large ones. It does not identify the cause.","p":63,"f":"SND (effect / SE) regressed on precision (1 / SE)"},{"q":"Trim and fill imputes three trials and the OR falls from 1.53 to 1.45. What does this mean?","a":"The benefit shrinks once apparent missing trials are allowed for, so the original is probably inflated. Imputed trials are hypothetical: a sensitivity check, not data.","p":64},{"q":"What does Rosenthal's fail-safe N estimate, and what does a large value imply?","a":"How many unpublished null studies would be needed to make the result non-significant. A large number suggests the finding is robust to hidden null studies.","p":62,"f":"fail-safe N = (ΣZ)² / 1.645² − k"},{"q":"A trial misses its primary outcome but the abstract highlights a significant secondary subgroup and calls the drug 'promising'. Name the reporting bias.","a":"Spin: presentation that makes a null result look favourable without changing the data. Read the primary outcome, not the abstract's framing.","p":65},{"q":"How can reviewers reduce publication bias at the search stage?","a":"Search trial registries, grey literature and regulators' clinical study reports, avoid English-only searches, and rely on prospective registration to expose unpublished trials.","p":60}]},{"t":"Galbraith and L'Abbé plots","s":66,"e":71,"c":[{"q":"What are the axes of a Galbraith (radial) plot?","a":"x = precision (1/SE), so big trials sit right; y = z score (estimate/SE). A line from the origin has slope equal to the pooled fixed-effect estimate.","p":67,"f":"z = estimate / SE"},{"q":"On a Galbraith plot, what does a trial lying outside the ±2 band mean?","a":"It is an outlier contributing disproportionately to heterogeneity. About 95% of trials should fall inside if all estimate one common effect.","p":67},{"q":"A Galbraith plot reveals an outlier trial. Should it be deleted?","a":"No. Investigate why (patients, dose, methods) and report a sensitivity analysis with and without it. Deleting awkward trials just to cut heterogeneity is unacceptable.","p":68},{"q":"A Galbraith plot for lithium shows the pooled line sloping downwards. Why, given relapse is the outcome?","a":"The slope equals the pooled log RR (−0.28, RR 0.76). Lithium reduces relapse, so log RR is negative and the line slopes down.","p":67},{"q":"What are the axes of a L'Abbé plot, and what does the diagonal show?","a":"x = control event rate, y = treatment event rate. The diagonal is the line of equality (y = x): no effect. Distance from it shows the effect.","p":66},{"q":"On a L'Abbé plot of relapse rates, which side of the line of equality indicates benefit?","a":"Below it: fewer relapses with treatment. For a good outcome such as response, benefit lies above. Always check whether the outcome is good or bad.","p":68},{"q":"What does the size of each circle on a L'Abbé plot represent?","a":"Trial size (number randomised), not effect size. The effect is the circle's distance from the line of equality.","p":68},{"q":"What is a sensitivity analysis in a meta-analysis?","a":"Repeating the analysis with different decisions, e.g. excluding high risk-of-bias trials or one study at a time, to test whether the conclusion is robust.","p":67},{"q":"A cumulative meta-analysis shows the CI excluding 1 from 2005, but seven more trials followed. What does this suggest?","a":"The answer was clear by 2005; later trials narrowed the interval without changing the conclusion, raising questions about whether they, and their placebo arms, were needed.","p":70},{"q":"What three features make a subgroup finding in a meta-analysis credible?","a":"Pre-specification, few comparisons tested, and a significant test for interaction between subgroups, ideally with biological plausibility, rather than comparing whether each subtotal is significant.","p":66}]},{"t":"Network meta-analysis","s":72,"e":77,"c":[{"q":"What does a network meta-analysis add over a standard pairwise meta-analysis?","a":"It combines direct and indirect evidence to compare every treatment in a network, including pairs never tested head to head, via common comparators.","p":72},{"q":"On a network plot, what do node size and edge width show?","a":"Node size: patients randomised to that treatment. Edge width (or number): how many trials directly compare the two treatments.","p":73},{"q":"No line joins Drug A and Drug E on a network plot. What does this mean?","a":"No head-to-head trials exist, not 'no difference'. A versus E can still be estimated indirectly, through placebo or other links.","p":74},{"q":"OR for response is 2.0 for Drug A versus placebo and 1.6 for Drug C versus placebo. Estimate the indirect OR for A versus C.","a":"1.25 (2.0 ÷ 1.6): a ratio of ratios through the common comparator. On the log scale this is a subtraction.","p":73,"f":"OR(A vs C) = OR(A vs P) / OR(C vs P)"},{"q":"Why is an indirect estimate less precise than a direct one of similar size?","a":"Its variance is the sum of both component variances, so uncertainty from each placebo comparison accumulates and the CI widens.","p":73,"f":"Var(indirect) = Var(A vs P) + Var(C vs P)"},{"q":"Define transitivity and give an example of its violation.","a":"Trials are similar enough in effect modifiers to be chained. Violated if Drug A's trials recruited more severe patients than Drug C's.","p":74},{"q":"What is inconsistency (incoherence) in a network, and where can it be checked?","a":"Disagreement between direct and indirect estimates of the same comparison. It can be tested in closed loops, e.g. by node-splitting.","p":74},{"q":"Drug E has a 62% probability of ranking first and the top SUCRA. Can you call it the best antidepressant?","a":"No. Rankings exaggerate small, uncertain differences. E rests on few small trials and its ORs versus C and D have CIs including 1.","p":76},{"q":"A league table cell, column C and row B, reads 1.21 (0.90 to 1.62); above 1 favours the column. Interpret it.","a":"OR 1.21 for C versus B, but the CI includes 1, so no clear difference. Reading the cell the wrong way gives the reciprocal (about 0.83).","p":75},{"q":"What does SUCRA stand for and what scale does it use?","a":"Surface under the cumulative ranking curve, 0 to 100%: higher means more likely to rank near the top. It must be read alongside effect estimates and certainty.","p":73}]},{"t":"Sensitivity and specificity","s":78,"e":84,"c":[{"q":"In a 2×2 table with index test in rows and reference standard in columns, write sensitivity and specificity.","a":"Sensitivity = a/(a + c), read down the disease column; specificity = d/(b + d), read down the no-disease column.","p":78,"f":"sensitivity = a / (a + c) · specificity = d / (b + d)"},{"q":"PHQ-9: 68 true positives, 12 false negatives, 32 false positives, 288 true negatives. Calculate sensitivity and specificity.","a":"85% and 90%. Sensitivity 68/80; specificity 288/320. Reading the rows instead gives predictive values (68% and 96%).","p":79,"f":"sensitivity = 68 / 80 · specificity = 288 / 320"},{"q":"Using the same PHQ-9 table (68, 12, 32, 288; N = 400), what is overall accuracy, and why is it not sensitivity?","a":"89%. Accuracy mixes both columns and is driven by the commoner group, here people without depression.","p":79,"f":"accuracy = (a + d) / N = 356 / 400"},{"q":"Explain SnNout and SpPin.","a":"A highly Sensitive test, when Negative, rules out because it rarely misses cases. A highly Specific test, when Positive, rules in because it rarely flags healthy people.","p":79},{"q":"Does sensitivity change when the test is used in a population with higher prevalence?","a":"No: it is calculated within the disease column. But it does change with case mix (spectrum). Predictive values, read along rows, change with prevalence.","p":80},{"q":"Lowering the PHQ-9 cut-off from 15 to 10 changes sensitivity and specificity how, and why?","a":"Sensitivity rises (56% to 87%) and specificity falls (98% to 90%). More cases are caught but more healthy people are flagged, because the distributions overlap.","p":80},{"q":"A MoCA cut-off gives 100% sensitivity and specificity in severe dementia versus healthy volunteers, but 92% and 67% in memory clinic referrals. Name the bias.","a":"Spectrum bias. Comparing extremes separates the groups artificially; in real clinics mild dementia overlaps with look-alikes such as MCI and depression.","p":81},{"q":"Only 10% of screen-negatives receive the reference interview, and analysis uses verified patients only. How are sensitivity and specificity distorted?","a":"Partial verification bias: sensitivity is overestimated and specificity underestimated, because most false and true negatives disappear. Weighting back corrects sensitivity 97.6% to 80%.","p":82},{"q":"EPDS: 34 true positives, 6 false negatives, 21 false positives, 189 true negatives. Calculate the false positive rate.","a":"10%: 21 of the 210 women without depression were wrongly flagged. It equals 1 − specificity (90%). Dividing by positive results instead gives 1 − PPV.","p":83,"f":"FPR = b / (b + d) = 21 / 210"},{"q":"Two tests with sensitivity 0.9 and 0.8 are used in series (positive only if both positive). What is the combined sensitivity?","a":"0.72. Serial testing multiplies sensitivities, so sensitivity falls while specificity rises. Parallel testing does the reverse.","p":83,"f":"Se(serial) = Se₁ × Se₂ = 0.9 × 0.8"}]},{"t":"Predictive values and prevalence","s":85,"e":89,"c":[{"q":"A patient asks what her positive screening result means. Which accuracy measure answers this, and how is it calculated from a 2×2 table?","a":"Positive predictive value: true positives divided by all positive results. It starts from the result (the row), whereas sensitivity starts from the truth (the column), so quoting sensitivity answers a different question.","p":85,"f":"PPV = a / (a + b)"},{"q":"A screen with sensitivity 80% and specificity 90% is used where prevalence is 10%. Working with 1,000 people, what is the PPV?","a":"About 47%. 100 have the condition and 80 test positive; 900 do not and 90 test positive falsely. So 80 of 170 positives are true: roughly half are false alarms.","p":86,"f":"PPV = TP / (TP + FP) = 80 / 170"},{"q":"A questionnaire moves from a specialist clinic to primary care. Its sensitivity and specificity are unchanged. What happens to PPV and NPV, and why?","a":"PPV falls and NPV rises. Where the condition is rarer, the large healthy group generates false positives that swamp the true ones, while negative results become even more trustworthy.","p":86},{"q":"Compute the PPV from sensitivity 90%, specificity 90% and prevalence 20% using Bayes' theorem.","a":"About 69%. True positives 0.9 × 0.2 = 0.18; false positives 0.1 × 0.8 = 0.08; PPV = 0.18 ÷ 0.26. This lets you recompute PPV for any local prevalence.","p":86,"f":"PPV = (Se × prev) / [Se × prev + (1 − Sp) × (1 − prev)]"},{"q":"A test has sensitivity 90% and specificity 90%. At a prevalence of 20%, what is its NPV?","a":"About 97%. True negatives 0.9 × 0.8 = 0.72; false negatives 0.1 × 0.2 = 0.02; NPV = 0.72 ÷ 0.74. NPV reads along the test-negative row of the table.","p":86,"f":"NPV = [Sp × (1 − prev)] / [Sp × (1 − prev) + (1 − Se) × prev]"},{"q":"A diagnostic accuracy study recruits 100 known cases and 100 controls and reports a PPV of 85%. What is wrong with applying this PPV in clinic?","a":"The design fixes prevalence at 50%, so the PPV is artificial and fits no real setting. Take sensitivity and specificity from the study and recompute PPV using the local prevalence.","p":87},{"q":"Why is a negative screening result less reassuring in a high-risk group than in the general population?","a":"NPV falls as prevalence rises. With more cases present, the missed ones (false negatives) make up a bigger share of all negatives, so a negative result leaves more residual risk.","p":87},{"q":"When screening for a rare condition, which test property matters most for an acceptable PPV, and why?","a":"Specificity. When nearly everyone tested is healthy, even a small false positive rate applied to that huge group yields more false positives than there are true cases.","p":85},{"q":"Of 1,000 people, 20 have a condition and 18 of them test positive. Of the 980 without it, 98 test positive. What is the PPV?","a":"About 16%: 18 of 116 positives are true, so 98 are false alarms. Natural frequencies make the base rate visible: the healthy majority supplies most positives even at 90% specificity.","p":87,"f":"PPV = 18 / (18 + 98)"},{"q":"A screen with sensitivity and specificity both 85% is used where prevalence is 2%. Roughly how many of every 100 positives are false?","a":"About 90. True positives 0.017 against false positives 0.147 gives a PPV near 10%. Universal screening for a rare condition mostly produces false alarms, so positives need careful assessment, not a label.","p":88,"f":"PPV = (0.85 × 0.02) / (0.85 × 0.02 + 0.15 × 0.98)"}]},{"t":"Likelihood ratios and Fagan nomogram","s":90,"e":95,"c":[{"q":"Define the positive likelihood ratio and state how it is calculated from sensitivity and specificity.","a":"How many times likelier a positive result is in someone with the condition than in someone without it. It does not depend on prevalence, so it transfers between settings better than PPV.","p":90,"f":"LR+ = sensitivity / (1 − specificity)"},{"q":"A test has sensitivity 90% and specificity 80%. Calculate LR+ and LR−.","a":"LR+ 4.5 (a small upward shift) and LR− 0.125 (a moderate downward shift). Both rest on sensitivity and specificity alone, so prevalence does not change them.","p":91,"f":"LR+ = 0.9 / 0.2; LR− = (1 − 0.9) / 0.8"},{"q":"The pre-test probability of bipolar disorder is 20%. A test result has an LR of 8. What is the post-test probability?","a":"About 67%. Convert 20% to odds (0.25), multiply by 8 to get post-test odds of 2, then convert back: 2 ÷ 3. Never multiply the probability itself.","p":91,"f":"post-test odds = pre-test odds × LR; probability = odds / (1 + odds)"},{"q":"A trainee says: pre-test probability 10%, LR 10, so the post-test probability is 100%. What is the error, and what is the right answer?","a":"He multiplied a probability rather than odds. Odds 0.111 × 10 = 1.11, which converts to about 53%. Odds can exceed 1; probabilities cannot, which is why the odds step matters.","p":92,"f":"probability = odds / (1 + odds) = 1.11 / 2.11"},{"q":"Which likelihood ratio values produce a large, often conclusive change in probability, and which rarely matter?","a":"Above 10 or below 0.1 shift probability a lot. Values from 0.5 to 2 rarely change anything. So an LR− of 0.4 is weak evidence, not strong.","p":91},{"q":"A biomarker moves from a memory clinic to primary care. Does its likelihood ratio change? What does?","a":"The LR does not, because it depends only on sensitivity and specificity. The post-test probability does change, because the starting pre-test probability differs between the two settings.","p":92},{"q":"How is a Fagan nomogram used, and what does a likelihood ratio of 1 look like on it?","a":"Lay a straight line from the pre-test probability (left) through the LR (middle) and read post-test probability on the right. An LR of 1 gives a horizontal line: the result changes nothing.","p":92},{"q":"For a fixed likelihood ratio, at what kind of pre-test probability does a test add most? What follows clinically?","a":"In the middle range of genuine uncertainty (about 30% for LR 5). When the diagnosis is nearly certain or nearly excluded, any result barely moves the probability, so test when unsure.","p":93},{"q":"A score band contains 30 of 100 cases and 10 of 200 non-cases. What is the band-specific likelihood ratio, and why use bands?","a":"LR 6: 30% of cases versus 5% of non-cases fall in the band. Multilevel LRs use the whole score range, keeping information that a single cut-off throws away.","p":94,"f":"LR(band) = proportion of cases in band / proportion of non-cases in band = 0.30 / 0.05"},{"q":"The pre-test probability of dementia is 40%. A negative result has LR− 0.1. What is the post-test probability?","a":"About 6%. Odds 0.4 ÷ 0.6 = 0.67; multiply by 0.1 to get 0.067; convert back: 0.067 ÷ 1.067. An LR− of 0.1 argues firmly against the diagnosis.","p":91,"f":"post-test odds = pre-test odds × LR−; probability = odds / (1 + odds)"}]},{"t":"ROC curves, AUC and cut-off","s":96,"e":101,"c":[{"q":"What is plotted on each axis of a ROC curve, and at which side is specificity highest?","a":"Sensitivity on the y-axis against 1 − specificity (false positive rate) on the x-axis. Specificity is highest at the left, where false positives are fewest. Labelling the x-axis plain specificity is a classic error.","p":96},{"q":"A risk score has an AUC of 0.85. Explain what this means in plain English.","a":"Pick one person with and one without the condition at random: 85% of the time the case has the more abnormal score (ties count half). It summarises all cut-offs and equals the c-statistic.","p":98},{"q":"Cut-off A gives sensitivity 84% and specificity 76%; cut-off B gives 70% and 88%. Which has the larger Youden index?","a":"Cut-off A: J = 0.84 + 0.76 − 1 = 0.60, against 0.58 for B. J is the vertical height above the chance diagonal, so the larger value sits nearer the curve's shoulder.","p":97,"f":"J = Se + Sp − 1"},{"q":"Missing a case would be dangerous and follow-up assessment is cheap. Which way along the ROC curve should the chosen cut-off move?","a":"Towards the top right: a lower, more lenient cut-off, buying sensitivity at the price of more false positives. The Youden point is only a default; the clinical cost of each error sets the operating point.","p":98},{"q":"What does the diagonal line on a ROC plot represent, and what is its AUC?","a":"A test with no discrimination: each gain in sensitivity costs an equal rise in false positives. Its AUC is 0.5, the same as chance.","p":98},{"q":"The positive threshold on a depression scale is raised from 8 to 14. What happens to sensitivity, specificity and LR+?","a":"Sensitivity falls, specificity rises and LR+ goes up. The operating point slides down and to the left along the curve: fewer false alarms, more missed cases.","p":96},{"q":"Why is the cut-off with the highest LR+ not automatically the best one to use?","a":"The strictest cut-off often has the top LR+ because false positives are rare, yet it misses most cases. A positive there is convincing, but a negative reassures little.","p":98},{"q":"A paper reports AUC 0.94 and says its test is 94% accurate at the recommended cut-off. Spot the flaw.","a":"AUC summarises discrimination across every cut-off. It is not the accuracy of any single threshold, does not tell you which cut-off to pick, and is unaffected by prevalence.","p":98},{"q":"Score distributions in people with and without a condition lie exactly on top of each other. What is the AUC, and what does the ROC curve look like?","a":"AUC 0.5: the curve is the diagonal. Any cut-off flags the same share of cases and non-cases. The further apart the two distributions, the closer the AUC gets to 1.","p":100},{"q":"On a ROC curve with several labelled cut-offs, which point misses the fewest cases, and which wrongly flags the fewest healthy people?","a":"The highest, rightmost point (most lenient cut-off) has the greatest sensitivity. The leftmost point (strictest cut-off) has the smallest false positive rate, so the highest specificity.","p":100}]},{"t":"Screening criteria and bias","s":102,"e":107,"c":[{"q":"What is screening, and which body advises the UK nations on population screening programmes?","a":"Offering a test to people without symptoms to identify those at higher risk who may benefit from further tests or treatment. The UK National Screening Committee advises the four UK nations.","p":102},{"q":"Screening diagnoses a condition at age 60 rather than 64, and death occurs at 75 either way. What is the lead time, and how does survival from diagnosis change?","a":"Lead time is 4 years. Survival from diagnosis rises from 11 to 15 years, yet death is unchanged: the clock simply started earlier. No real benefit has been shown.","p":103,"f":"lead time = age at clinical diagnosis − age at screen detection = 64 − 60"},{"q":"Screen-detected cases survive longer from diagnosis but die at the same average age as clinically detected cases. Name the bias and the clue.","a":"Lead-time bias. The identical age at death gives it away: earlier diagnosis adds apparent survival without postponing death. Effective treatment would move death later.","p":103},{"q":"Why might cases found at a single screening round have a better prognosis even if screening achieves nothing?","a":"Length-time bias. Slowly progressing illness has a long detectable preclinical phase, so it is more likely to be present on screening day. Screen-detected cases are enriched for slower, milder disease.","p":104},{"q":"Define overdiagnosis in screening and explain why it flatters a programme.","a":"Finding disease that would never have caused symptoms or harm in the person's lifetime. These cases boost numbers detected and apparent survival while exposing people to labels and treatment they never needed.","p":104},{"q":"Which outcome, from which study design, gives a fair test of whether a screening programme works?","a":"Mortality counted from randomisation in an RCT of screening versus no screening. The clock starts identically in both arms and all cases count, defeating lead-time and length-time bias; all-cause mortality also captures harms.","p":105},{"q":"Must a condition be curable before screening for it meets the Wilson and Jungner principles?","a":"No. The principle asks for an accepted treatment, not a cure. Others include an important health problem, a recognisable early stage, a suitable and acceptable test and understood natural history.","p":104},{"q":"A perinatal depression screen would refer positives to a perinatal team that has no capacity to see them. Which Wilson and Jungner principle fails?","a":"Facilities for diagnosis and treatment must be available. A programme that finds people it cannot assess or treat promptly adds anxiety and harm without benefit.","p":106},{"q":"Nobody knows which children with subthreshold attention problems go on to develop ADHD. Which screening principle would an early-detection programme fail?","a":"The natural history must be adequately understood. Without knowing who progresses, nobody can say who would gain from early detection, so screening risks labelling children who would have been fine.","p":106},{"q":"Why can a suicide risk tool with good sensitivity and specificity not reliably predict suicide in individual patients?","a":"Suicide is rare, so prevalence and therefore PPV are very low: almost everyone flagged as high-risk will not die by suicide. The same logic means screen positives always need confirmation.","p":102}]},{"t":"Validity of measures","s":108,"e":112,"c":[{"q":"A scale gives identical scores every time it is repeated. Does that make it valid?","a":"No. Reliability (consistency) is necessary but not sufficient for validity (measuring the right thing). Bathroom scales that always read 2 kg heavy are perfectly consistent and consistently wrong.","p":108},{"q":"A new anxiety scale correlates r = 0.80 with an established anxiety measure and r = 0.05 with shoe size. What do these results support?","a":"Construct validity: convergent (high correlation with a similar measure) and divergent or discriminant (near-zero correlation with an unrelated one). Together they show scores behave as theory predicts.","p":108},{"q":"What is face validity, who judges it, and how strong is it as evidence?","a":"Whether the items appear, to those using the scale such as patients, to measure what they claim. It is a judgement rather than a statistic, and the weakest form of validity.","p":109},{"q":"Experts check that a new eating disorder scale covers restriction, bingeing, compensatory behaviour and body image. Which validity is being assessed?","a":"Content validity: expert judgement that every relevant domain is sampled. With face validity it makes up logical validity, assessed by inspecting the items rather than by correlations.","p":111},{"q":"Distinguish concurrent from predictive validity, giving an example of each.","a":"Both are criterion validity. Concurrent: a new depression scale against a structured diagnostic interview at the same time. Predictive: childhood ADHD ratings against later school exclusion.","p":109},{"q":"A scree plot shows eigenvalues 4.1, 2.3, 1.4, 0.8 and 0.6 for the first five factors. How many factors does the Kaiser criterion retain?","a":"Three: only eigenvalues above 1 are kept. Items grouping into the intended dimensions supports construct validity. The elbow of the plot is a second way to count factors.","p":110},{"q":"A new depression scale correlates r = 0.59 with an anxiety scale. Does this undermine its validity?","a":"Not necessarily. Depression and anxiety overlap, so a moderate correlation is expected. It should be clearly lower than the correlation with another depression measure, showing the scale separates the constructs.","p":111},{"q":"A paper cites a high Cronbach's alpha and a high test-retest ICC as proof that its scale is valid. What is the flaw?","a":"Both are reliability statistics, measuring internal consistency and stability. They show the scale is consistent, not that it measures the intended construct. Validity needs judgement, other measures or a criterion.","p":111},{"q":"What does criterion validity require that construct validity does not?","a":"An external criterion: a gold standard such as a structured diagnostic interview at the same time (concurrent), or a later outcome (predictive). Construct validity instead tests theory-based predictions against other measures.","p":108},{"q":"Name the three main branches of validity and say how each is assessed.","a":"Logical (face, content): judged by inspecting items. Construct (convergent, divergent, factor structure): tested against theory. Criterion (concurrent, predictive): compared with a gold standard or future outcome.","p":109}]},{"t":"Reliability: kappa, ICC, Bland-Altman","s":113,"e":117,"c":[{"q":"Two raters classify 100 patients: both yes 45, both no 40, A yes and B no 5, A no and B yes 10. Calculate Cohen's kappa.","a":"κ = 0.70, substantial. Observed agreement is 0.85. Chance agreement from the margins is (50 × 55 + 50 × 45) ÷ 100² = 0.50. So κ = 0.35 ÷ 0.50.","p":114,"f":"κ = (Po − Pe) / (1 − Pe)"},{"q":"Why is raw percentage agreement a poor measure of inter-rater reliability?","a":"It ignores agreement expected by chance. If both raters say 'no' most of the time they will often agree by luck; kappa strips out that chance component.","p":113},{"q":"State the Landis and Koch descriptors for values of kappa.","a":"0.20 or less slight; 0.21 to 0.40 fair; 0.41 to 0.60 moderate; 0.61 to 0.80 substantial; above 0.80 almost perfect.","p":114},{"q":"Two raters' PANSS totals correlate r = 0.95, but the absolute-agreement ICC is 0.86. What explains the difference?","a":"One rater scores systematically higher. Correlation only asks whether the points fall on a straight line, so a constant shift costs nothing; the absolute-agreement ICC penalises it. Correlation is not agreement.","p":115},{"q":"How is a Bland-Altman plot constructed, and what do its lines show?","a":"Plot the difference between two methods against their mean. The central line is the mean difference (bias); the limits of agreement, bias ± 1.96 SD, contain 95% of differences.","p":114,"f":"limits of agreement = mean difference ± 1.96 × SD of differences"},{"q":"Comparing two lithium assays, the mean difference is 0.05 mmol/L and the SD of differences 0.04 mmol/L. What are the 95% limits of agreement?","a":"About −0.03 to +0.13 mmol/L: 0.05 ± 0.078. Whether the assays are interchangeable is a clinical judgement about that range, not a significance test.","p":114,"f":"limits = 0.05 ± 1.96 × 0.04"},{"q":"A 20-item scale has a mean inter-item correlation of 0.2. What is its standardised Cronbach's alpha, and is it acceptable?","a":"About 0.83, acceptable (0.7 or above). Numerator 20 × 0.2 = 4; denominator 1 + 19 × 0.2 = 4.8. Remember alpha climbs with item count alone.","p":116,"f":"α = k × r̄ / [1 + (k − 1) × r̄]"},{"q":"A new scale reports Cronbach's alpha of 0.96. Why might this be a concern rather than a triumph?","a":"Above about 0.9, items are probably redundant, asking the same thing repeatedly. Alpha also rises just by adding items, so a high value does not prove the scale is unidimensional.","p":115},{"q":"Five crisis team members each classify the same referrals as urgent or routine. Which reliability statistic fits, and what if they gave continuous scores?","a":"Fleiss' kappa, for more than two raters making categorical ratings. For several raters giving continuous scores, use the intraclass correlation coefficient, which also penalises systematic differences between raters.","p":117},{"q":"Distinguish test-retest, inter-rater and intra-rater reliability.","a":"Test-retest: same measure, same people, two occasions. Inter-rater: different raters, same patients. Intra-rater: one rater scoring the same material twice. Each asks whether results hold steady when nothing has changed.","p":113}]},{"t":"Appraising prognosis studies","s":118,"e":123,"c":[{"q":"What is the best design for a prognosis study, and why must everyone be recruited at a common early point?","a":"A prospective inception cohort. Recruiting people at varying stages misses those who did badly early, so the survivors make the outlook look rosier than it is (survivor bias).","p":118},{"q":"A prognosis cohort loses 25% of participants to follow-up. How concerned should you be?","a":"Seriously. Under 5% lost is reassuring; over 20% threatens validity, because people who drop out often fare differently from those who stay.","p":118},{"q":"On a Kaplan-Meier plot, what does the y-axis show, and what does a small vertical tick mean?","a":"The proportion still event-free, not the proportion who had the event. A tick is a censored person: they left or reached study end without the event and count as at risk only until then.","p":119},{"q":"How do you read median survival from a Kaplan-Meier curve, and what if the curve is still above 0.5 when follow-up ends?","a":"Run across from 0.5 to the curve and drop down. If the curve never reaches 0.5, the median is not reached and cannot be determined; quoting the end of follow-up invents a number.","p":119,"f":"Median survival = first time S(t) ≤ 0.5"},{"q":"Survival just before an event time is 0.80. Fifty people are at risk and 5 relapse. What is the Kaplan-Meier survival estimate afterwards?","a":"0.72. Each step multiplies current survival by the share of those at risk who escaped the event, here 45 of 50.","p":119,"f":"S(t) = S(previous) × (1 − d / n) = 0.80 × (1 − 5 / 50) = 0.72"},{"q":"What does the log-rank test give you, and what does Cox regression add?","a":"Log-rank compares whole survival curves without adjustment and gives a p value. Cox regression gives a hazard ratio adjusted for other prognostic factors, assuming proportional hazards.","p":119},{"q":"A paper reports a hazard ratio of 2 for relapse. A colleague says twice as many relapse by the end. Correct them.","a":"HR 2 means double the instantaneous risk of relapse at any moment among those still well. It does not mean twice the cumulative events, nor relapsing twice as fast.","p":120},{"q":"Survival curves for two maintenance drugs cross at about 12 months. Why would a single Cox hazard ratio mislead?","a":"The proportional hazards assumption fails. One drug does worse early and better later, so one HR averages opposite effects. Report time-specific effects or restricted mean survival time instead.","p":121},{"q":"A curve rises from 0% to 29% at 24 months in an at-risk mental state cohort. What is plotted, and what is the transition rate?","a":"Cumulative incidence of psychosis, the mirror image of survival. Read the y value: 29% had transitioned by 24 months. It never reaches 50%, so no median time can be given.","p":122,"f":"Cumulative incidence = 1 − S(t)"},{"q":"In a study of time to dementia diagnosis, many older participants die first. Why is treating these deaths as ordinary censoring wrong?","a":"Death is a competing risk: it stops dementia ever occurring. Censoring assumes the person could still develop it later, so the cumulative incidence of dementia is overestimated.","p":122}]},{"t":"Reporting guidelines: CONSORT, STARD, PRISMA","s":124,"e":132,"c":[{"q":"A trial report ticks every CONSORT item. Does that mean it has a low risk of bias?","a":"No. Reporting guidelines check what was reported, not how well the study was done; a well-reported trial can still be biased. Judge bias with a tool such as Cochrane RoB 2. EQUATOR catalogues the guidelines.","p":124},{"q":"Which guideline covers the report of a randomised trial, and which covers its protocol?","a":"CONSORT 2025 (30 items) for the report; SPIRIT 2025 (34 items) for the protocol. Swapping the two is a favourite name trap.","p":124},{"q":"Which guideline should a new systematic review of RCTs follow, which one is historical, and what covers meta-analyses of observational studies?","a":"PRISMA 2020 (27 items). QUOROM was replaced by PRISMA in 2009 and is now history. Meta-analyses of observational studies use MOOSE.","p":125},{"q":"An EPDS validation against a structured clinical interview in 600 women: STROBE or STARD?","a":"STARD 2015, for diagnostic accuracy of an index test against a reference standard. STROBE covers observational cohort, case-control and cross-sectional studies. The near-identical names are the trap.","p":128},{"q":"PRISMA: 1,846 database and 212 register records, 604 duplicates, 1,362 excluded at title and abstract, 4 not retrieved. How many full texts were assessed?","a":"88. Work down the left column: 1,454 screened, 92 sought, 88 assessed. Each exclusion box plus the next box down equals the box above.","p":125,"f":"Assessed = (1,846 + 212 − 604) − 1,362 − 4 = 88"},{"q":"CONSORT diagram: 120 allocated to app-based CBT-I; 14 lost and 9 discontinued. What percentage did not complete follow-up?","a":"19.2%, using the arm's own allocated number as denominator, not all 240 randomised. The leaflet arm lost 8.3%; unequal attrition between arms raises the risk of attrition bias.","p":127,"f":"Non-completion = (14 + 9) / 120 = 19.2%"},{"q":"Which reporting guidelines suit qualitative research, economic evaluations and quality improvement work?","a":"Qualitative: COREQ or SRQR. Economic evaluation: CHEERS 2022. Quality improvement, such as plan-do-study-act cycles: SQUIRE 2.0. Name the design first, then the checklist.","p":124},{"q":"Which three CARE items for case reports are most often forgotten, and why do case reports matter?","a":"A timeline of key events, the patient's own perspective and informed consent. One case cannot show causation, but complete reports flag rare adverse effects and unusual presentations.","p":126},{"q":"A psychotherapy trial omits who delivered sessions, how often, and with what fidelity. Which guideline fixes this?","a":"TIDieR: 12 items describing an intervention well enough for someone else to deliver it, including who, how, where, when and how much, tailoring and fidelity. Used alongside CONSORT and SPIRIT.","p":126},{"q":"An audit finds only 38% of trials report allocation concealment. Why does this matter to reviewers?","a":"Concealment protects against selection bias. When it is unreported, reviewers must rate bias as unclear or some concerns, weakening the evidence even if the trial was done properly.","p":128}]}];
